      "text": "Explain the historical circumstances that led John Locke and other political philosophers to develop political ideas such as those expressed in his Second Treatise of Government.",
      "maxPoints": 1,
      "modelAnswer": "During the Enlightenment, as ideas spread through Europe and the Scientific Revolution encouraged applying reason and natural law to society, philosophers like John Locke responded to the oppressive power and abuses of absolute, divine-right monarchs by questioning traditional authority and developing theories of government based on natural rights and the consent of the governed.",
      "explanation": "Full credit (1 point) for a correct historical circumstance behind Locke's ideas. Acceptable responses (NYSED rating guide) include: Enlightenment ideas spreading in Europe; the Enlightenment leading people to question the nature of political power; oppressive government tyranny denying basic liberties, leading to theories based on reason/natural law; philosophers recognizing abuses of government and spreading ideas for change; Locke responding to absolute monarchs abusing power; the Scientific Revolution influencing thinkers to apply natural law to politics; questioning government traditions in England; questioning divine-right theory/absolute monarchy; thinkers putting faith in reason; rational assessments of how government functioned; or determining the relationship between ruler and ruled. Not acceptable: 'French Revolution,' 'American Revolution,' 'Locke lived in the Dark Ages,' 'Locke wanted to end monarchies,' 'Locke believed in natural rights/consent of the governed,' 'tyranny,' 'abuse of power,' 'theories of government,' or 'questioning attitude.'",
      "image": "/images/exams/global-history/january-2020/context_29_29.png",
      "context": "Base your answer to question 29 on Document 1 below and on your knowledge of social studies.\\nDocument 1\\nThis excerpt is taken from John Locke’s Second Treatise of Government, which was \\noriginally published in 1690.\\n. . .95. MEN being, as has been said, by nature, all free, equal, and independent, no one \\ncan be put out of this estate, and subjected to the political power of another, without his \\nown consent. The only way whereby any one divests [surrenders] himself of his natural \\nliberty, and puts on the bonds of civil society, is by agreeing with other men to join and \\nunite into a community for their comfortable, safe, and peaceable living one amongst \\nanother, in a secure enjoyment of their properties, and a greater security against any, \\nthat are not of it. This any number of men may do, because it injures not the freedom of \\nthe rest; they are left as they were in the liberty of the state of nature. When any number \\nof men have so consented to make one community or government, they are thereby \\npresently incorporated, and make one body politic, wherein the majority have a right to \\nact and conclude the rest. . . .\\nSource: C. B. Macpherson, ed., John Locke, Second Treatise of Government,\\nHackett Publishing Company"
